WEBVTT

00:00.220 --> 00:01.649
This is Axion Kitchen.

00:01.799 --> 00:06.898
We first solved the factory baselines: optimal schedules of four and
eight hours.

00:07.048 --> 00:09.480
Our extension schedules catering production.

00:09.630 --> 00:13.491
Cookers are machines, cleaning is setup, and departures are deadlines.

00:13.641 --> 00:19.956
We choose shared batches, qualified workers and timings, while
respecting freshness, calendars and outages.

00:20.106 --> 00:21.514
First, serve complete meals.

00:21.664 --> 00:25.141
Then reduce waste, extra batches and changes to the plan.

00:26.220 --> 00:28.140
Two customers need ten meals each.

00:28.290 --> 00:33.794
Main A is already cooking, and one batch of twenty side portions can
serve both orders.

00:33.944 --> 00:39.832
Here's the conflict: that side batch and one Main B option need cooker
C2 at the same time.

00:39.982 --> 00:43.545
QAOA selects a different Main B schedule on C1.

00:43.695 --> 00:47.151
Both orders get a main and a side, packed before departure.

00:47.301 --> 00:49.583
Sharing the side batch saves making it twice.

00:50.220 --> 00:51.585
Remove just the side batch.

00:51.735 --> 00:53.527
Neither order gets a complete meal.

00:53.677 --> 00:55.469
Twenty main portions go to waste.

00:55.619 --> 00:58.265
That's why we optimize meals, rather than dishes.

01:00.220 --> 01:05.959
Each bit selects a batch program: how much to make, which orders it
serves, who does the work, and when.

01:06.109 --> 01:11.549
Programs competing for the same demand sit in one block; we choose
one, or leave it idle.

01:11.699 --> 01:13.960
Penalties rule out clashes between blocks.

01:14.110 --> 01:16.286
The code builds those pair terms directly.

01:16.436 --> 01:19.956
This small case has thirty-two programs and three idle bits.

01:20.106 --> 01:23.135
A restricted set of programs can miss better schedules.

01:26.220 --> 01:30.359
Multiply main coverage by side coverage to count complete meals.

01:30.509 --> 01:33.559
Their sum minus twice that product counts unpaired food.

01:33.709 --> 01:37.635
Coverage is linear in our bits, so both expressions stay quadratic.

01:37.785 --> 01:41.689
Our weights make each priority outweigh all lower costs combined.

01:41.839 --> 01:48.751
For constraints, we bound how much the objective can vary across all
bitstrings, then set the penalty one higher.

01:48.901 --> 01:50.714
That proves feasible ground states.

01:50.864 --> 01:52.933
It doesn't guarantee a solver finds them.

01:54.220 --> 01:56.780
We map the QUBO to an Ising Hamiltonian.

01:56.930 --> 01:58.999
The mixer preserves block occupation.

01:59.149 --> 02:04.675
Fixing the rest of the schedule turns global interactions into local
fields for VeloxQ.

02:06.220 --> 02:09.178
An outage also changes what happens after repair.

02:09.326 --> 02:10.713
The cooker still needs cleaning.

02:10.861 --> 02:16.488
Here, the worker must finish committed work first, then clean from
eleven forty to eleven fifty.

02:16.636 --> 02:18.269
Both resources stay reserved.

02:18.417 --> 02:23.724
Our checker rebuilds those intervals from the schedule, along with
freshness and delivery checks.

02:24.220 --> 02:27.804
Let's run the saved depth-three QAOA circuit locally.

02:27.954 --> 02:31.346
Cost and conflict phases alternate with an XY mixer.

02:31.496 --> 02:36.189
The mixer keeps one choice per block, but clashes between blocks still
need checking.

02:36.339 --> 02:40.606
We draw four thousand ninety-six fresh samples, using the saved
angles.

02:40.756 --> 02:42.825
The result on the right comes from this run.

02:42.975 --> 02:46.623
The kitchen animation is a separate replay of a saved result.

02:49.220 --> 02:50.287
Does training help?

02:50.437 --> 02:56.751
We chose the settings on the small cell and S0/S1, then tested S2/S3.

02:56.901 --> 03:00.208
Training finds the best available plan in all ten seeds.

03:00.358 --> 03:02.961
Random-angle search also hits ten on S2.

03:03.111 --> 03:10.919
At two hundred fifty-six evaluations, training gives roughly eleven
and twenty-two times the optimum probability per shot.

03:11.069 --> 03:13.949
We optimize the angles separately for each instance.

03:14.099 --> 03:17.405
And these limited program sets miss better full-grid plans.

03:18.220 --> 03:21.684
VQE uses Givens and controlled Givens rotations.

03:21.832 --> 03:27.776
Its median probability of the offered-pool optimum is about twelve
percent in each disruption case.

03:27.923 --> 03:34.905
Using CVaR and adjoint gradients, it samples that optimum in ten seeds
out of ten, and nine out of ten.

03:35.053 --> 03:37.767
Its budgets and shots differ from QAOA.

03:38.220 --> 03:41.889
For larger plans, VeloxQ sits inside a replanning loop.

03:42.039 --> 03:43.469
It starts from its own plan.

03:43.619 --> 03:51.256
We free two blocks, solve their conditional QUBO, and keep a change
only if it passes the checker and improves the objective.

03:51.406 --> 03:57.443
Across three sizes, missed meal lots drop from nine to seven, eleven
to six, and thirteen to seven.

03:57.593 --> 03:59.364
That took seventy-one submissions.

03:59.514 --> 04:04.805
Exact neighborhood controls do as well or better, and longer classical
runs still win.

04:06.220 --> 04:07.872
The small cell tests correctness.

04:08.018 --> 04:13.578
Larger inputs generate eighteen thousand batch programs with shared
resources and demand.

04:13.724 --> 04:17.766
We measure the cost of restricting choices separately from solver
quality.

04:18.220 --> 04:19.543
What capacity should we add?

04:19.693 --> 04:22.594
Our best current-capacity plan serves ninety meals.

04:22.744 --> 04:26.008
A third cooker still serves ninety, but removes the waste.

04:26.158 --> 04:28.782
A prep or packing helper reaches one hundred ten.

04:28.932 --> 04:31.279
The same worker currently does both jobs.

04:31.429 --> 04:34.863
Each variant gets the same classical budget and two seeds.

04:35.013 --> 04:37.509
These are feasible plans, not proven optima.

04:37.659 --> 04:40.305
We'd need prices before recommending a purchase.

04:42.220 --> 04:46.803
Our log records why we changed cleaning, meal coverage and idle
encoding.

04:46.952 --> 04:49.113
Twenty meals, no waste in the small case.

04:49.262 --> 04:51.732
The demo links to code, proofs and benchmarks.
